		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=42935#p42935"><![CDATA[
Thanks Avik,<br><br>I had a look and found a few issues. One of them is in the applyExternalForce imlpementation, I fixed this now.<br><a href="https://github.com/bulletphysics/bullet3/pull/2870/commits/ebef331bbffb4631259648f5858a3a396dd3718a" class="postlink">https://github.com/bulletphysics/bullet ... 396dd3718a</a><br><br>(it was a copy/paste bug, we never used applyExternalForce, since we always actuate our robots using actuators/motors)<br><br>In your code:<br>1) disable position/velocity motors before applying an external torque/force<br>2) use a reasonably timestep, 1 is not OK. Use 1./240 or at largest 1./60.<br>3) always provide collision shapes in the URDF, since PyBullet derives the inertia tensor from mass and collision volume by default<br><br>It is also easier to debug when the force is applied around the link world center-of-mass location.<br>See attached zip file for my updates.<br>Thanks for the report!<dl class="file"><dt><span class="imageset icon_topic_attach"></span> <a class="postlink" href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/download/file.php?id=1830">avik_examples.zip</a></dt></dl><p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=2">Erwin Coumans</a> — Fri Jun 19, 2020 3:48 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
